From d5091a901857861dca1c9f04b13d19c5c33f2ef2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexanderNyr <alexander@auralite.local>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 09:32:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] X6 call isolation: ABI v3 (env3), a vault, an attacker and a
 chain
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A contract can call another contract, and the two rules that make that safe
live in the host rather than in the SDK.

ABI v3 is a third import module, env3 — call, returndata_len, returndata_read,
depth — for the same reason env2 was a second one: env and env2 are frozen by
the 23 X2 vectors, the 43 X5 tests and every deployed contract, and a module
declares the surface it speaks by what it imports. The u128 value of a call is
passed as two i64 halves, because a pointer would let calldata alias with it.

The rules: a frame may not call an address already on the stack (-14), and a
stack may not exceed abi_v3::MAX_CALL_DEPTH = 8 (-15). Reentrancy is checked in
admit(), before a child store exists, so a contract cannot talk its way past it.
That is stricter than a per-account lock and much simpler: the classic
withdraw-then-reenter attack becomes unrepresentable rather than detected. The
cost is named in docs/CALLS.md — a callback into a live frame does not exist on
this chain, so a receiver is notified forward, after the money has moved.

A child frame gets its own store, its own budget drawn from the parent's
remainder, and its own base version. On success its diff is merged and its
events appended; on failure its writes, events and value are gone and the caller
reads the status. That is the difference between a transaction and a frame: X2
commits any Returned(_), but a callee's answer must reach its caller unchanged,
which is why -21 from a callee's dispatcher now arrives as -21 and not as 0.

Three contracts, each its own workspace, each built twice into independent
target directories and pinned by sha256 and by the node's own BLAKE3 over the
shipped bytes: vault (9 759 B, pays before it debits — the bug X6 exists to
survive), evil (11 607 B, re-enters from the payout callback, probes, sends
value, records statuses instead of trapping), recurse (9 093 B, a chain of nine
copies; a contract may not call itself, so self-recursion is not expressible).

Measured through the real Lifecycle: vault deploy 107 369, evil deploy 117 100,
recurse deploy 100 132, fund 41 188, attack 87 697, honest withdrawal after the
attack 35 116, nine-frame descent 28 414. The attacker gets one attempt, its
ledger is debited once, the vault ends empty rather than short, and the honest
depositor is paid her 9 800 in full.

Gates: 15 tests in x6_calls, 311 in the workspace, 6/6 X6 negative controls,
147 in total, 4 692 vectors across 24 fixtures, 239 external crates — X6 adds no
dependency at all, and docs/evidence/x6-dependency-addendum.txt records that.

Fixed: the wasmi backend kept its own copy of the allowed import modules and
refused env3; a child's status was dropped, so a callee's -14 reached its caller
as 0; the vault's first draft paid through env3.call, which cannot pay an account
with no code; and the first depth fixture called itself, which the guard
correctly refuses — the fixture had to become a chain of distinct contracts.
